course: dictionary
lesson: 13692
title: Inoffensive: harmless or plain
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Inoffensive» is a word for something that will not upset, annoy or hurt anyone. When you meet it, look at the words around it. «The hamster was an inoffensive little pet that never bit anyone.» The clue is never bit anyone, so inoffensive means harmless and gentle.
explain: The word can also describe something that is pleasant but a bit dull. «The walls were an inoffensive beige.» Nothing about the colour is exciting, but nobody would complain about it. Ask yourself: is this thing harmful, or just ordinary? The clues tell you which meaning fits.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If you read «The joke was harmless» and it still makes sense, you have got it right.
fact: Inoffensive means not likely to upset, annoy or offend anyone.
fact: Inoffensive also means pleasant but rather dull or ordinary, like «an inoffensive colour».
fact: «Inoffensive» is an adjective, and its comparative form is more inoffensive.
fact: In stories, an inoffensive character is often quiet and harmless, and sometimes is picked on unfairly.
exercise: «The hamster was an inoffensive little pet that never bit anyone.» What does inoffensive mean? | *Harmless and gentle | Expensive and rare | Very loud and noisy | Angry and fast
exercise: «The walls were an inoffensive beige.» What does inoffensive mean here? | Dangerous to touch | Very tall | *Pleasant but rather dull | Bright and shiny
exercise: «Ben told an inoffensive joke that made nobody cross.» Find and copy the word that means the same as inoffensive. | told | *inoffensive | nobody | joke
test: «The quiet boy was inoffensive, so the bullies picked on him unfairly.» What does this tell us about the boy? | *He never upset anyone, so being picked on was unfair | He was loud and rude | He was always angry | He was the best at sport
test: «The inoffensive shade of grey did not stand out at all.» What does inoffensive suggest here? | Angry with the room | *Pleasant but ordinary, not eye-catching | Shocking and bright | Cold and wet
exam: «Mum said the film was perfectly inoffensive, so it was fine for the little ones.» What does perfectly inoffensive mean? | *Completely harmless, nothing to upset anyone | Very scary and loud | Full of sad songs | Hard to understand
